#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# boolean_function

#% text
# `boolean_function(k, n)` is the Boolean function of `n` variables whose truth table is given by the binary digits of `k`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Bit `i` of `k` is the value at the assignment whose binary number is `i`: false is 0, true is 1, the first argument is the highest bit. So `boolean_function(1, 1)` is `not x`, `boolean_function(8, 2)` is `x and y`.

#% item
# `boolean_function(k)` uses the fewest variables that hold `k`; `boolean_function(table)` takes the list of values in the order of `boolean_table` (all true first); a value that is neither `true` nor `false` does not matter and the smallest sum of products that agrees is used. `boolean_function([[b1, ..., bn] -> v, ...])` takes rules; the assignments not named do not matter.

#% item
# `boolean_function(spec, [x, y, ...])` is the sum of products in those variables.

#% item
# `f(a, b, ...)` evaluates to `true` or `false` when every argument is; with some of them `true` or `false` it is the function of the others, in their remaining positions; otherwise it stays.

#% item
# `boolean_convert` accepts calls of these functions and `"bff"` makes one from any expression.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
f = boolean_function(30, 3)
f(true, false, true)
#% output
#= false

#% text
# As an expression in variables:

#% cell
boolean_function(30, [x, y, z])
#% output
#= x and not y and not z or not x and y or not x and z

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# From a table (all true first, as `boolean_table` lists it):

#% cell
boolean_function([true, false, false, true])
#% output
#= boolean_function(9, 2)

#% text
# Fixing some arguments:

#% cell
boolean_function(30, 3)(true, x, false)
#% output
#= boolean_function(1, 1)(x)

#% text
# From rules, with the other assignments not mattering:

#% cell
boolean_function([[true, true, false] -> true, [false, true, true] -> false], [x, y, z])
#% output
#= not z

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The truth table of a function:

#% cell
boolean_table(boolean_function(30, 3)(a, b, c), [a, b, c])
#% output
#= [false, false, false, true, true, true, true, false]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `boolean_function(5)` takes the fewest variables that hold the bits (a table of 4 entries):

#% cell
[boolean_function(5), boolean_function(0), boolean_function(15, 2)]
#% output
#= [boolean_function(5, 2), boolean_function(0, 1), boolean_function(15, 2)]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A table whose length is not a power of 2 and a negative number give failures:

#% cell
[boolean_function([true, false, false]), boolean_function(-1, 2)]
#% output
#= [failure(argument, boolean_function, 1), failure(argument, boolean_function, 1)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `boolean_convert`, `boolean_counting_function`, `boolean_table`
